  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 PM EDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nInitial area of showers and thunderstorms in eastern IA moved\neast and has been falling apart as it becomes orphaned from the\nbetter low level flow (850 mb winds up to 50 kts at times).\nAdditional convection has been forming behind it, generally\ndrifting in a SE direction. Over time CAMs show this area\nweakening as the LLJ relaxes some and wave departs. The same\nCAMs seem to suggest new develop not in IA but closer to the\narea (eastern IL/SW MI/NW IN) later tonight as the LLJ begins to\nshift somewhat and weak isentropic lift occurs ahead of a\nslightly better moisture flux. Will continue with slgt chc to\nchc pops into Sunday morning, but the vast majority of locations\nwill likely remain dry. \n\nThereafter our rain chances will be dictated by the position of\nthe edge of a building upper level ridge from the south. Sadly,\nmuch like past weeks we will be on the northern fringe of the\nridge which could bring some chances for showers and storms mid\nto late week as an upper level low tries to edge in but is\nlikely deflected NE. One difference this time around may be the\naxis of best precip ends up north and we end up on the warmer\nand more humid side, especially Tuesday and Wednesday. Signals\npoint towards highs quite likely at or above 90 degrees with\nafternoon heat indices in the upper 90s to around 100. Something\nto keep in the back of your mind and monitor for now.",
